# CCAO-F Practice Set 01 — Claude Certified Associate – Foundations

Written against the official Exam Guide v1.0 (July 2026). Original practice items, not exam
content.
60 questions · 120 minutes · domain-grouped · ~60% Advanced / ~40% Expert · 8 select-TWO
items.
Every option is a real practice; wrong options are right in a nearby situation — the key
says where.

| Domain | Weight | Items |
|---|---|---|
| 1 Prompting and Task Execution | 14% | 8 |
| 2 Output Evaluation and Validation | 21% | 13 |
| 3 Product and Model Selection | 12% | 7 |
| 4 Workflow Integration and Solution Design | 16% | 10 |
| 5 Configuration and Knowledge Management | 12% | 7 |
| 6 Governance, Risk, and Responsible Use | 15% | 9 |
| 7 Troubleshooting and Optimization | 10% | 6 |

---

## Domain 1: Prompting and Task Execution (14%)

### Question 1.1 · Advanced · Multiple choice · select ONE

An HR coordinator asks Claude to "draft an onboarding checklist for new hires" and gets a
generic result. She has the company's two-page orientation document and knows this cohort is
remote field technicians starting next week. Which revision most improves the first pass?

**A.** Add a role line: "You are an experienced HR onboarding specialist at a mid-size
      firm."

**B.** Ask for a longer, more detailed checklist with sub-steps under every item.

**C.** Attach the orientation document and state the hires are remote field technicians.

**D.** Ask Claude to list the clarifying questions it needs answered before drafting.

### Question 1.2 · Expert · Multiple choice · select ONE

A project manager asks Claude for a one-paragraph status update for this week's stakeholder
email. The content is accurate and complete, but the tone is slightly too formal. A
colleague suggests restructuring the request. What is the best next step?

**A.** Give one targeted instruction to make the tone more conversational and keep the rest.

**B.** Decompose it into separate requests for progress, risks and next steps, then combine.

**C.** Regenerate the paragraph several times and keep the version with the best tone.

**D.** Add three past status paragraphs in the team's preferred tone as examples.

### Question 1.3 · Advanced · Multiple choice · select ONE

A training lead needs about 30 icebreaker ideas for a hybrid workshop and will pick five
later with her team. Which prompting approach fits this stage of the task?

**A.** Specify a fixed template per idea: name, duration, materials, steps, success
      criteria.

**B.** Ask for the five best activities with a justification for why each beats the rest.

**C.** Give the workshop's learning objectives and ask Claude to rank activities against
      them.

**D.** Ask for many varied ideas across formats and energy levels, and shortlist afterward.

### Question 1.4 · Expert · Multiple choice · select ONE

An operations lead uploads documentation for four vendors and asks for a comparison memo
across five criteria. The single-prompt result is accurate, but two vendors are covered in
depth and two only thinly. Which approach most reliably fixes the coverage?

**A.** Ask Claude to rewrite the memo so that every vendor section is the same length.

**B.** Have Claude build a vendor-by-criteria table first, check it, then draft the memo
      from it.

**C.** Run four separate chats, one per vendor, and paste the four results together.

**D.** Ask Claude to identify the thinly covered vendors and expand those sections.

### Question 1.5 · Advanced · Multiple choice · select ONE

Claude drafts a policy FAQ with the right content, but each answer runs 150+ words. The
target is under 60 words, and an earlier "please be concise" changed little. What is the
most effective next iteration?

**A.** Repeat the instruction with emphasis: "Be MUCH more concise in every answer."

**B.** Ask Claude to cut every answer by half while keeping the most important points.

**C.** Set a hard 60-word limit per answer and show one rewritten answer as the model.

**D.** Start a new chat with the same prompt and "concise" added to the opening line.

### Question 1.6 · Expert · Multiple choice · select ONE

A consultant must draft a change-management plan for a client business unit she knows little
about. She isn't yet sure which information about the unit will matter for the plan. What is
the best first prompting move?

**A.** Describe the engagement and ask Claude to interview her with the questions a plan
      needs.

**B.** Ask for a complete plan using a standard framework, then edit it to fit the client.

**C.** Provide a template with every section heading and ask Claude to fill in each section.

**D.** Ask Claude to summarize best-practice change frameworks before starting the plan.

### Question 1.7 · Advanced · Multiple choice · select ONE

An associate pastes three months of complaint categories and counts. Leadership must decide
which single issue to fund a fix for next quarter. Which request yields the most
decision-useful analysis?

**A.** "Summarize the complaint data and describe the main patterns you notice."

**B.** "Chart complaint volume by category for each of the three months."

**C.** "List every category that grew, ordered from the largest increase to the smallest."

**D.** "Recommend one issue to fund, weighing volume, growth and impact, and show why."

### Question 1.8 · Advanced · Multiple response · select TWO

A communications lead will reuse one prompt every week to turn engineering release notes
into a customer newsletter section. Which TWO prompt elements most improve week-to-week
consistency? (Select TWO.)

**A.** One or two past newsletter sections the team approved, included as target examples

**B.** An instruction to vary the structure each week so readers don't find it repetitive

**C.** A request that Claude rate how closely each draft matches the brand voice

**D.** Explicit include/exclude rules, such as skipping internal-only fixes and tooling
      changes

**E.** A persona line casting Claude as an award-winning newsletter editor

