## Domain 3: Product and Model Selection (12%)

### Question 3.1 · Advanced · Multiple choice · select ONE

An associate needs one paragraph reworded for clarity before sending an email today. A
colleague suggests setting up a Project for it. What fits best?

**A.** A Project, so the rewording preferences are saved for consistency later.

**B.** Research mode, to check the wording against current clarity guidelines.

**C.** An artifact, so the paragraph can be edited and versioned in place.

**D.** A standard chat, since a one-off edit needs no persistent setup.

### Question 3.2 · Expert · Multiple choice · select ONE

Colleagues keep asking about the company's 2026 travel policy. Answers must come only from
that internal document; public web guidance often conflicts with it. What setup fits best?

**A.** Research mode, so each answer comes with sources colleagues can check.

**B.** A Project with the policy as knowledge and an instruction to answer only from it.

**C.** A standard chat, pasting the policy in at the start of each question.

**D.** Research mode, with an instruction to prefer company sources over others.

### Question 3.3 · Advanced · Multiple choice · select ONE

An associate must, once, reconcile three conflicting consultant reports into a
recommendation the CEO will act on. Which model choice fits?

**A.** Haiku, run several times, keeping the most persuasive answer.

**B.** Sonnet, since it balances cost and quality for everyday work.

**C.** Opus, since this is a one-off, complex reasoning task where quality dominates.

**D.** Haiku for the first draft, then Opus to polish the final wording.

### Question 3.4 · Expert · Multiple choice · select ONE

A team classifies 5,000 tickets nightly into six clear categories. A pilot showed Haiku and
Opus agree on 99% of tickets, Haiku at a fraction of the cost; the 1% are genuinely
ambiguous tickets. What is the best setup?

**A.** Haiku for all tickets, with an "unclear" option routed to human review.

**B.** Opus for all tickets, since it handles the ambiguous 1% better.

**C.** Sonnet for all tickets, as a compromise between cost and quality.

**D.** Haiku for all tickets, then re-run every ticket on Opus to catch errors.

### Question 3.5 · Advanced · Multiple choice · select ONE

Deep into a long chat analyzing a 200-page report, Claude starts missing details from the
early sections. What is the best response?

**A.** Switch to the most capable model so the whole report stays in view.

**B.** Paste the full report into the same chat again to refresh it.

**C.** Ask Claude to pay closer attention to the early sections of the report.

**D.** Work section by section in focused chats, carrying a verified summary forward.

### Question 3.6 · Expert · Multiple choice · select ONE

An associate with Memory enabled made key decisions over several chats. A colleague takes
over the work next week from their own account. How should the decisions be made available?

**A.** Rely on Memory, since it carries project context into future chats.

**B.** Add a verified decision summary to the shared Project's knowledge.

**C.** Share the chat links so the colleague can read the decisions there.

**D.** Ask Claude to remember the decisions so it can recall them later.

### Question 3.7 · Advanced · Multiple response · select TWO

Which TWO outputs are best developed as artifacts rather than inline chat answers? (Select
TWO.)

**A.** A customer onboarding guide the team will revise and reuse

**B.** A one-paragraph policy explanation to paste into an email reply

**C.** An interactive budget calculator to share with department heads

**D.** A five-row comparison the associate will glance at once

**E.** Feedback on a draft that the associate will apply right away

